<ul class="rs-grid"><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/the-architecture-of-power"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/games/behistun-inscription.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 01</span><span class="rs-title">The Architecture of Power</span><span class="rs-sub">How Empires Build the Structures That Sustain Them</span><span class="rs-desc">An examination of the administrative, legal, and infrastructural systems that allowed large-scale polities to persist, and why their absence almost always preceded collapse.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">8 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/trade-and-territory"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 02</span><span class="rs-title">Trade and Territory</span><span class="rs-sub">Economic Networks Across the Ancient and Medieval World</span><span class="rs-desc">A study of how control over trade routes, currency systems, and agricultural surplus shaped imperial ambition, and how economic disruption so often proved fatal to imperial order.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">10 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/the-collapse-problem"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persepolis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 03</span><span class="rs-title">The Collapse Problem</span><span class="rs-sub">Common Patterns in Imperial Decline Across Five Millennia</span><span class="rs-desc">Why do empires fall? Comparing the trajectories of the Western Roman Empire, the Abbasid Caliphate, the Mongol Empire, and others to identify recurring structural vulnerabilities.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">17 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 04</span><span class="rs-title">Cross-Cultural Exchange</span><span class="rs-sub">How Empires Spread Ideas and Culture, and Were Changed by Them</span><span class="rs-desc">From Hellenisation to the Islamic Golden Age to the Columbian Exchange, this series explores the two-way nature of cultural contact under imperial conditions and its lasting consequences.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">14 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/the-military-revolution"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/games/battle-of-mohacs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 05</span><span class="rs-title">The Military Revolution</span><span class="rs-sub">Technology, Tactics, and the Shifting Balance of Power</span><span class="rs-desc">How advances in military technology — the stirrup, gunpowder, the warship — reshaped the competitive landscape between polities and enabled otherwise peripheral states to become world powers.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">14 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/who-should-rule"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-senate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 06</span><span class="rs-title">Who Should Rule?</span><span class="rs-sub">One, Few or Many: Forms of Government Compared</span><span class="rs-desc">From Athenian assemblies and Venetian councils to absolute kings and juntas: how twenty forms of government answered Aristotle's questions of who should rule and for whose good, and what the record shows.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">17 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/before-the-state"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lapita-pottery.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 07</span><span class="rs-title">Before the State</span><span class="rs-sub">How Peoples Lived Without Kings, and Some Still Do</span><span class="rs-desc">From the Natufian villages of the Levant and the Jōmon potters of Japan to the Māori, Mapuche, Sámi, Ainu and Taíno of today: how peoples ordered their lives without a state, and what forty-five cultures show beside a register of 5,468 states.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">19 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/how-songs-travel"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 08</span><span class="rs-title">How Songs Travel</span><span class="rs-sub">The Evolution, Branching and Power of Song</span><span class="rs-desc">From the Lament for Ur to Glory to Hong Kong: how 300 songs kept through history passed from hymn to anthem, lent their tunes across borders, were put to work by states and parties, and outlived the powers that sang or banned them.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">19 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philae.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 09</span><span class="rs-title">Faiths on the Move</span><span class="rs-sub">How Thirty Religions Spread, Met and Ended</span><span class="rs-desc">From the city gods of Sumer to the Bahá'í Faith: how thirty religions began, were carried by kings, merchants, missionaries, soldiers and the enslaved, met and borrowed from one another, and came in some places to their last temple.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">19 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/who-invented-it-first"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clay-tokens.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 10</span><span class="rs-title">Who Invented It First?</span><span class="rs-sub">Firsts, Credit and the Long Road of Ideas</span><span class="rs-desc">From stone tools 3.3 million years ago to the World Wide Web: what 291 inventions and discoveries show about firsts made more than once, credit given and withheld, named inventors and anonymous makers, and why &quot;first&quot; is so often disputed.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">19 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/games/code-of-hammurabi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 11</span><span class="rs-title">From Ruler to Enslaved</span><span class="rs-sub">What Fifty Social Ladders Show Side by Side</span><span class="rs-desc">From Hammurabi's Babylonia to Saint-Domingue and Stalin's Soviet Union: how fifty societies ranked their people, who was owned or bound, how people rose and fell, and how uncertain the numbers are.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">19 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/the-written-word"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/games/rosetta-stone.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 12</span><span class="rs-title">The Written Word</span><span class="rs-sub">How Thirty-Two Languages Were Written, Carried, Outlived Their States and Came Back</span><span class="rs-desc">From the accounts of Uruk to the schools that brought back Hebrew and Hawaiian: how thirty-two languages were first written, carried by empires and faiths, outlived their states and were read again.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">19 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/midwinter"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winter-solstice.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 13</span><span class="rs-title">Midwinter</span><span class="rs-sub">How the World Has Marked the Darkest Days</span><span class="rs-desc">From Saturnalia and Yule to Dongzhi, Yalda Night and Inti Raymi: how societies have marked the winter solstice and the weeks around it, and how their feasts, gift-bringers and songs borrowed from, replaced and outlived one another.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">20 min read</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="rs-card" href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history"><span class="rs-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-truce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy"></span><span class="rs-b"><span class="rs-num">Series 14</span><span class="rs-title">Christmas Day in History</span><span class="rs-sub">Crownings, Truces and Crossings on 25 December</span><span class="rs-desc">From Aurelian's temple to the Sun in 274 to Gorbachev's resignation in 1991: how a midwinter feast became a day for crowning kings, for crossing rivers by night, for laying down arms, and for carols and stories.</span><span class="rs-foot"><span class="rs-cta">Read the essay &rarr;</span><span class="rs-time">19 min read</span></span></span></a></li></ul>
